INT. NIGHT FERRY, LOWER CAR DECK - NIGHT

Rows of parked cars. Chains rattle. Fog presses on the stern gate. A single fluorescent tube hums over three folding chairs where the deckhands drink coffee.

LORCAN BRENNAN-OSEI (50s, deckhand) pours from a thermos. NELLWYN PRYCE-ADEYEMI (20s, new hire, hi-vis vest) sits on the chair edge. THADDEUS "TAD" MBEKI-LINDEN (60s, captain's patch on his sleeve) rubs his eyes.

LORCAN
You hear that?

NELLWYN
Hear what?

LORCAN
Knocking.

TAD
Engine.

LORCAN
Not the engine. Knocking. Under us.

NELLWYN
Under the deck?

LORCAN
Under the hull.

TAD
Lorcan, it's the sea.

LORCAN
The sea doesn't knock three times.

NELLWYN
Maybe it's a buoy.

TAD
Maybe it's a buoy.

LORCAN
It's not a buoy.

TAD
Then what is it?

LORCAN
Last crossing, it knocked four times. Before that, five.

NELLWYN
Counting down?

LORCAN
Counting something.

TAD
Stop frightening the girl.

NELLWYN
I'm not frightened.

TAD
Then you've not been listening.

LORCAN
Three. That's three. Tonight it's three.

NELLWYN
And after three?

LORCAN
Two, one.

NELLWYN
And after one?

LORCAN
That's what I'm asking him.

TAD
(low)
After one, we don't dock.

Silence. The fluorescent tube flickers. The fog at the stern gate seems to lean.

NELLWYN
That's a joke.

TAD
It's a rule. My father taught me. When it counts down to one, you turn the ship around.

NELLWYN
Tonight is three.

LORCAN
Tonight is three.

TAD
Then pray for a long crossing.

A single knock, soft, from under the deck.

NELLWYN
That was two.

LORCAN
That was two.

TAD
(barely)
Turn her around.

LORCAN
Captain?

TAD
Turn. Her. Around.
